WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:04.200
All Pixar movies take a long time to develop,
even by Hollywood standards.

00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:09.030
The studio spends years building their lavishly
rendered stories, but even for Pixar, the

00:00:09.030 --> 00:00:12.640
journey Coco took from idea to blockbuster
was a rather lengthy one.

00:00:13.120 --> 00:00:16.840
So what took so long, what changed, and how
did we arrive at the film we now know and

00:00:16.840 --> 00:00:17.340
love?

00:00:18.340 --> 00:00:19.080
Day of the Dead

00:00:19.840 --> 00:00:24.220
Co-director Lee Unkrich was fresh off the
triumph of Toy Story 3 when the idea for Coco

00:00:24.220 --> 00:00:26.780
came to him, at Walt Disney World, of all
places.

00:00:27.150 --> 00:00:30.960
According to Unkrich, he was in the middle
of a boat ride at Epcot's Mexico pavilion

00:00:30.960 --> 00:00:33.730
when he saw an image that ignited a spark
of inspiration.

00:00:34.200 --> 00:00:34.760
He recalled:

00:00:35.160 --> 00:00:38.620
"We passed a mariachi skeleton band, and that
was my trigger moment.

00:00:38.620 --> 00:00:42.300
I started to think about the potential of
telling a story set against the culture of

00:00:42.300 --> 00:00:42.800
Mexico.

00:00:43.060 --> 00:00:45.940
I had never seen a full story told against
Dia de los Muertos."

00:00:46.500 --> 00:00:50.620
Pixar approved the project and Unkrich started
moving forward, but Coco was different from

00:00:50.620 --> 00:00:51.840
other Pixar productions.

00:00:52.240 --> 00:00:53.000
Unkrich said:

00:00:53.240 --> 00:00:57.100
"When I did Toy Story 3, we settled on a few
basic ideas that were going to be part of

00:00:57.100 --> 00:00:57.600
the film.

00:00:57.640 --> 00:01:01.100
And of course we had most of our characters
already set, and the look of the world.

00:01:01.540 --> 00:01:02.670
In this case, we had nothing.

00:01:02.840 --> 00:01:05.200
I mean, we just had a blank piece of paper
and that was it."

00:01:06.020 --> 00:01:07.020
Public backlash

00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:12.700
Though Coco is very heavily rooted in Mexican
culture, it began as the brainchild of a white

00:01:12.700 --> 00:01:15.660
director who had a deep desire to ensure its
authenticity.

00:01:16.120 --> 00:01:21.100
Those efforts took a turn toward PR nightmare
in 2013, when Disney attempted to trademark

00:01:21.100 --> 00:01:22.640
the phrase "Dia de los Muertos."

00:01:23.300 --> 00:01:24.979
"Don't give me that look.

00:01:25.260 --> 00:01:30.080
Dia de los Muertos is the one day of the year
our ancestors can come visit us!"

00:01:30.500 --> 00:01:35.140
Prominent Latinos, including cartoonist Lalo
Alcaraz, saw it as a cold attempt by an American

00:01:35.149 --> 00:01:39.479
corporation to appropriate a cornerstone of
Mexican culture, and they were outspoken in

00:01:39.480 --> 00:01:40.200
their criticism.

00:01:40.660 --> 00:01:45.159
The backlash prompted Pixar to relax its usually
rather secretive production process and hire

00:01:45.160 --> 00:01:50.240
several Latino "cultural consultants" to work
on the film, including Alcaraz, who said:

00:01:50.440 --> 00:01:53.920
"Pixar was already on its way to making this
a culturally authentic film, and we met somewhere

00:01:54.420 --> 00:01:54.980
in the middle.

00:01:55.180 --> 00:01:58.100
And even though I'm not very corporate, they
listened to what I had to say."

00:01:58.460 --> 00:02:02.399
The consultants provided input on every phase
of the production process, from character

00:02:02.400 --> 00:02:04.160
designs all the way through to test screenings.

00:02:05.420 --> 00:02:06.420
Looming finality

00:02:07.080 --> 00:02:10.980
After getting the green light to pursue his
idea, Unkrich and the production team began

00:02:10.980 --> 00:02:14.940
a thorough research process in an attempt
to absorb every detail about the Day of the

00:02:14.940 --> 00:02:15.440
Dead.

00:02:15.580 --> 00:02:19.280
It was then that they came across the idea
of "final death" — the notion that you die

00:02:19.290 --> 00:02:23.349
in the mortal world, and die again in the
afterlife when your memory in the land of

00:02:23.349 --> 00:02:25.040
the living is finally extinguished.

00:02:25.760 --> 00:02:30.080
According to Unkrich, this film-defining idea
was initially just a small part of the story.

00:02:30.230 --> 00:02:33.720
"At the time [we learned about it], we thought
it was a poignant idea and it was something

00:02:33.720 --> 00:02:34.700
we wanted in the movie.

00:02:35.320 --> 00:02:39.569
But after some time we came to the realization
that no, that's the idea of the movie which

00:02:39.569 --> 00:02:41.600
needed to permeate everything in the film."

00:02:42.120 --> 00:02:43.820
Secrets of the shrine

00:02:44.460 --> 00:02:48.420
One of the most important moments in the film
comes when Miguel retreats into a little hideaway

00:02:48.420 --> 00:02:52.640
he's built for himself where he can secretly
indulge in his true musical passions.

00:02:53.160 --> 00:02:57.980
The tiny room functions as a miniature shrine
to his musical idol Ernesto de la Cruz, featuring

00:02:57.980 --> 00:03:02.650
photos and albums, Miguel's own battered guitar,
and a small TV where Miguel can play a video

00:03:02.650 --> 00:03:05.420
compilation he's made of key de la Cruz moments.

00:03:06.060 --> 00:03:09.700
It's a vital scene because it reveals just
how serious Miguel is about music.

00:03:10.180 --> 00:03:14.200
The clearest inspiration for the scene may
have come from co-director Adrian Molina's

00:03:14.200 --> 00:03:16.280
own childhood dreams of being an animator.

00:03:16.560 --> 00:03:21.260
Molina recalled watching taped reruns of The
Wonderful World of Color on the Disney Channel:

00:03:21.420 --> 00:03:25.460
"These episodes would rerun at 4 a.m., and
I remember waking up every morning, sticking

00:03:25.470 --> 00:03:29.290
in a videocassette, and crossing my fingers
that it would record one of the episodes where

00:03:29.290 --> 00:03:31.300
they would talk about how a film was animated."

00:03:31.510 --> 00:03:35.640
Like Miguel, Molina cherished every snippet
of his heroes he could scrape together, and

00:03:35.660 --> 00:03:36.880
that made its way into the movie.

00:03:37.280 --> 00:03:38.959
"Aye aye aye, muchacho.

00:03:38.960 --> 00:03:41.780
I asked for a shoe shine, not your life story."

00:03:42.920 --> 00:03:43.700
Why Coco?

00:03:44.360 --> 00:03:48.260
If you're approaching Coco for the first time,
you might spend a good bit of the film a bit

00:03:48.260 --> 00:03:49.460
confused over the title.

00:03:49.900 --> 00:03:54.480
Its namesake, Miguel's great-grandmother,
is revealed fairly early on, but her significance

00:03:54.480 --> 00:03:58.900
to the plot is unclear until much later, when
the name takes on a great emotional weight.

00:03:59.409 --> 00:04:03.120
But for much of the production process it
was used only as a placeholder — a working

00:04:03.120 --> 00:04:05.820
title for the filmmakers until they came up
with a better one.

00:04:06.200 --> 00:04:07.200
Unkrich revealed:

00:04:07.200 --> 00:04:10.260
"We didn't know what we were going to call
the film for a very long time.

00:04:10.610 --> 00:04:14.340
We made lists of hundreds of different titles
to try and figure out what it could be.

00:04:14.340 --> 00:04:15.930
Coco was always just kind of a code name."

00:04:16.900 --> 00:04:17.980
Pixar innovation

00:04:18.640 --> 00:04:23.410
Each Pixar film presents an opportunity for
a potential new first in the field of animation,

00:04:23.410 --> 00:04:24.680
and Coco is no exception.

00:04:25.060 --> 00:04:29.040
The numerous skeletons in the film presented
several challenges, which became a problem

00:04:29.040 --> 00:04:31.880
when animators realized their software wasn't
up to the task.

00:04:32.360 --> 00:04:36.290
When clothing was placed on the skeletons
in the old program, it didn't react properly,

00:04:36.290 --> 00:04:38.220
and would get pinched and tangled between
bones.

00:04:38.950 --> 00:04:43.640
As a result, Pixar spent three years revamping
its software to accommodate the film, specifically

00:04:43.640 --> 00:04:46.320
focusing on how their animated objects would
interact.

00:04:46.820 --> 00:04:50.200
By the time they were done, the skeletons
didn't just wear clothes, they wore them tight

00:04:50.200 --> 00:04:53.540
against their ribs — just like real walking,
talking skeletons might.

00:04:54.600 --> 00:04:57.280
"*sigh* I miss my nose."

